# Separate actual mixed-P/D profiler diagnostic

This package complements the frozen `group-prefill-v1` no-profiler performance
family. It has no latency or throughput estimator. It profiles the same warmed
four P128/D512 seed requests and P12000/D128 ingress, with identical frozen token
IDs and sampling parameters. Raw and grouped runs use clean public plugin f7,
the same pinned Core/Ascend trees and HCCL_DETERMINISTIC=true. Their engine
configurations differ only in `compile_prefill_groups`; the graph backend remains
`enable_npugraph_ex=false`, `enable_static_kernel=false` on both sides.

The unchanged timing producer and helper are copied dependencies, bound by
`timing-execution-identity-v1.json`. No older producer, workload plan, result or
report is replaced. The unused native/native-async files remain provenance for
that exact copied timing bundle; this profile CLI accepts only raw or group.

## Fixed capture boundary and receipts

1. Complete one unprofiled, identical mixed workload warmup and drain the engine.
2. Admit four seed requests through native `LLMEngine.add_request` with DELTA
   output and stream interval1. Read actual returned tokens after each step.
3. After all four return exactly32 tokens, start the ordinary native worker
   profilers, obtain four active rank/PID/device/environment receipts, then admit
   the single frozen P12000 ingress request.
4. Capture until that ingress returns its first token. Record actual scheduler
   P-cohort layer progress and seed-D returns for every step in memory. Obtain
   post-capture counters, stop profiling, and require all four workers to report
   inactive profilers. No further engine step occurs inside the profiler.
5. Finish the remaining fixed outputs without profiling, then drain and shut
   down. All five requests must reach their exact ignore-EOS output budgets;
   no retry, omission or truncated successful result is allowed.

The existing workload bounds remain2048 steps and300 seconds per window. The
root controller bounds the whole fresh process at3000 seconds, plus bounded
owned-process cleanup. Profiler start/stop/export overhead can cause a failure
under these finite limits; retain that attempt rather than extending it silently.

Native Ascend `TorchNPUProfilerWrapper` uses CPU+NPU activities, Text export,
Level1, PipeUtilization and data simplification; stack and memory collection are
disabled. Exact source:
`vllm_ascend/profiler/torch_npu_profiler.py` at Ascend03766ac. Existing ordinary
worker classes are retained. The synchronous scheduler-state read is diagnostic
instrumentation, outside the separate no-profiler timing family.

## What an actual trace must prove

Successful collection alone is not an audited profile. `audit_mixed_profile.py`
requires all of the following before reporting a passing capture:

- Four distinct trace paths and file hashes, native `profiler_info_<rank>.json`,
  exact rank sets0..3, stable actual worker PIDs, and CPU-op PIDs in the traces.
- Four distinct observed hardware labels bound through the native rank/PID
  receipts. Visible worker ordinal is recorded separately and is never assumed
  equal to the host `npu-smi` physical ID.
- Actual P-layer advancement with same-step seed-D token returns. Grouped runs
  must record exactly one successful call to each of ten P groups on every rank;
  raw runs must have no grouped-P executor.
- Retained request/token arrays and every contiguous event must agree with the
  exact capture boundaries, one-token DELTA histories and final output lengths.
  Creating a zero-layer pending cohort does not count as P progress; the single
  ingress cohort must keep its identity and advance monotonically.
- Positive native D-controller replay deltas on all ranks, no capture/fallback/
  uncaptured/failure deltas, and actual exported
  `AscendCL@aclmdlRIExecuteAsync` counts exactly equal to those deltas.
- One profiling-enable and one profiling-disable marker, no graph-capture APIs,
  and stopped worker profilers before unprofiled completion.

The exact replay API is deliberately fail-closed. If this backend exports a
different API, retain the complete inventory and failed audit, inspect the
actual native source/export, then version any revised auditor explicitly.

The raw/group comparison additionally requires identical actual P-progress and
D-return signatures, capture boundaries, per-rank replay counts, source/runtime
identities and observed hardware labels. Config normalization removes only the
group flag and profiler output directory. A changed execution path fails the
matched-capture comparison; it cannot be silently treated as equivalent work.

The report includes every exported AscendCL runtime API's count and inclusive
host duration distribution, including zero-duration calls, plus signed
group-minus-raw count/duration differences. Inclusive API durations can overlap
or include waiting; their sum is not end-to-end wall time. This mixed window
cannot isolate P-only device time or prove Cube/Vector utilization limits. The
profiler also perturbs readiness and scheduling. Only the separate no-profiler
ABBA family can establish performance changes; the older pure-D16 profile does
not establish grouped-P execution benefit.

## Root-owned commands

Only root may run hardware, under the existing exclusive four-card lease. The
controller preserves the host venv interpreter symlink, copies every frozen
dependency, owns one fresh process group, records before/after physical device
inventories, and fails any timeout, forced cleanup or residual worker. It emits
the standard source/config/plan custody fields for the frozen capsule builder.

```bash
python run_controlled_group_profile.py dry-run --role raw --label check-mixed-profile-raw
python run_controlled_group_profile.py dry-run --role group --label check-mixed-profile-group
python run_controlled_group_profile.py run --role raw --label profile-f7-raw-mixed-01
python run_controlled_group_profile.py run --role group --label profile-f7-group-mixed-01
```

After successful collection, discover each actual rank's
`ASCEND_PROFILER_OUTPUT/trace_view.json` under its own attempt. Supply the paths
explicitly; filename order is not accepted as rank identity.

```bash
python audit_mixed_profile.py capture --run RAW/results/run.json --trace 0=RAW_RANK0_TRACE --trace 1=RAW_RANK1_TRACE --trace 2=RAW_RANK2_TRACE --trace 3=RAW_RANK3_TRACE --output NEW-raw-mixed-audit.json
python audit_mixed_profile.py capture --run GROUP/results/run.json --trace 0=GROUP_RANK0_TRACE --trace 1=GROUP_RANK1_TRACE --trace 2=GROUP_RANK2_TRACE --trace 3=GROUP_RANK3_TRACE --output NEW-group-mixed-audit.json
python audit_mixed_profile.py compare --raw NEW-raw-mixed-audit.json --group NEW-group-mixed-audit.json --output NEW-mixed-comparison.json
```

CPU fixtures exercise true P progress, exact start/stop gates, fixed completion,
stop failure, duplicate trace rejection, worker/hardware binding, zero-duration
API counting and unequal actual replay-count rejection. These are producer/audit
checks, not hardware observations. No model or device was launched during this
package preparation.
